  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q24-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "24",
    "prompt": "Doug went to a conference in a city 120 km away. On the way back, due to road construction, he had to drive 10 km/h slower, which resulted in the return trip taking 2 hours longer. How fast did he drive on the way to the conference?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$30\\text{ km/h}$. Use $120=st$ and $120=(s-10)(t+2)$ to obtain $(s-30)(s+20)=0$; take $s>10$.",
      "worked": "Let $s$ be the speed to the conference in km/h and $t$ the outward journey time in hours. Since the return speed is positive, $s>10$.\n\nUsing $d=st$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n120&=st\\\\\n120&=(s-10)(t+2)\\\\\n120&=st+2s-10t-20\\\\\n120&=120+2s-10t-20\\\\\n2s-10t-20&=0\\\\\nt&=\\frac{s-10}{5}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute into $120=st$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n120&=s\\left(\\frac{s-10}{5}\\right)\\\\\n600&=s(s-10)\\\\\ns^2-10s-600&=0\\\\\n(s-30)(s+20)&=0\\\\\ns&=30\\text{ or }-20.\n\\end{align*}$$\nReject $-20$ since $s>10$. Doug drove at $30\\text{ km/h}$ on the way to the conference.\n\nCheck: the outward journey took $120\\div30=4$ hours and the return journey took $120\\div20=6$ hours, which is 2 hours longer.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 95
  },
